### Notable cases in the wild
* [glob](https://docs.rs/glob/0.3.1/src/glob/lib.rs.html#1013) - `default()` and `new()` are actually different - likely was a bug that glob team wants to fix (per comments)
* [backtrace](https://docs.rs/backtrace/0.3.73/src/backtrace/symbolize/gimli.rs.html#318) - `new()` creates cache with `Vec::with_capacity()`, whereas `default()` uses `Vec::default()`. Possibly a bug.
* [tracing_core](https://docs.rs/tracing-core/0.1.32/src/tracing_core/subscriber.rs.html#705) uses `Self(())` -- should this be ignored?
* [tracing_subscriber](https://docs.rs/tracing-subscriber/0.3.18/src/tracing_subscriber/layer/mod.rs.html#1906) similarly uses `Self { _p: () }` for `struct Identity { _p: () }` -- probably better to use `::default()`

### TODO/TBD
* [x] Which cases of manual `fn new() -> Self` implementations are OK? (rather than delegating to `Self::default()`)
  * [x] In case `Self` is a unit type
* [x] given an `rustc_hir::hir::ImplItem` of a `fn new() -> Self`, get the body of that function
* [x] verify that the fn body simply does `return Self::default();` and no other logic (in all variants like `Default::default()`, etc.
* [x] I did a minor modification to `clippy_lints/src/default_constructed_unit_structs.rs` while trying to understand its logic - I think we may want to have a `is_unit_type(ty)` utility function?
* [ ] Handle generic types. This should be done in another PR due to complexity. See [comment](https://github.com/rust-lang/rust-clippy/pull/14234#issuecomment-2666824499)

